### What is the Strategy pattern?

Strategy has three parts: a strategy interface that declares the algorithm, concrete strategies that implement it differently, and a context that holds a strategy and delegates to it. The context depends only on the interface, so you can swap strategies without changing the context.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Strategy interface
public interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay(BigDecimal amount);
}

// Concrete strategies
public class UpiPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " via UPI");
    }
}

public class CardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " via Card");
    }
}

public class NetBankingPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " via Net Banking");
    }
}

// Context
public class PaymentContext {
    private PaymentStrategy strategy;

    public PaymentContext(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    public void setStrategy(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    public void checkout(BigDecimal amount) {
        strategy.pay(amount);
    }
}
```

### How does Strategy enforce the Open/Closed Principle?

Open for extension means you can add a new payment method. Closed for modification means you do not touch the tested code to do it. Without Strategy, a payment method would be a growing switch.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// VIOLATION of OCP: every new method edits this switch
public class PaymentProcessorBad {
    public void pay(String type, BigDecimal amount) {
        if ("UPI".equals(type)) {
            System.out.println("Paid via UPI: " + amount);
        } else if ("CARD".equals(type)) {
            System.out.println("Paid via Card: " + amount);
        }
        // adding WALLET means editing here
    }
}
```

With Strategy, adding `WalletPayment` is a new class that implements `PaymentStrategy`. The context and existing strategies are never touched.

```java
// New strategy – zero edits to existing code
public class WalletPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " via Wallet");
    }
}
```

### Where does Strategy appear in the Java standard library?

Strategy is everywhere in the JDK:

- **`Comparator`** is the clearest example. `list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance))` plugs a comparison algorithm into a fixed sort.
- **`RejectedExecutionHandler`** in `ThreadPoolExecutor` is a strategy for what to do when the queue is full.
- **`Collector`** in the Streams API is a strategy for how to accumulate stream elements.
- **`Runnable`** and **`Callable`** passed to a thread or executor are strategies for what work to do.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class StrategyInJdk {
    record Account(String name, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = Arrays.asList(
            new Account("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("Priya", new BigDecimal("250000")),
            new Account("Pankaj", new BigDecimal("50000"))
        );

        // Comparator is a Strategy
        accounts.sort(Comparator.comparing(Account::balance));
        accounts.forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.name() + ": " + a.balance()));
    }
}
```

```text
Pankaj: 50000
Rajesh: 150000
Priya: 250000
```

### How do you implement Strategy with lambdas in modern Java?

A single-method interface is a `@FunctionalInterface`, so a strategy can be a lambda or method reference instead of a named class.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StrategyLambdaDemo {
    @FunctionalInterface
    interface InterestStrategy {
        BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Strategies as lambdas – no named class needed
        InterestStrategy savings = balance -> balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
        InterestStrategy current = balance -> balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
        InterestStrategy nre     = balance -> balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.065"));

        BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("500000");
        System.out.println("Savings interest: " + savings.calculate(balance));
        System.out.println("Current interest: " + current.calculate(balance));
        System.out.println("NRE interest:     " + nre.calculate(balance));
    }
}
```

```text
Savings interest: 20000.000
Current interest: 10000.000
NRE interest:     32500.000
```

### How does Strategy work with Spring injection?

In Spring, injecting a `Map<String, PaymentStrategy>` gives runtime selection by bean name for free.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

@Component
public class PaymentService {
    private final Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies;

    // Spring injects all PaymentStrategy beans, keyed by bean name
    public PaymentService(Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies) {
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void pay(String method, BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentStrategy strategy = strategies.get(method);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown payment method: " + method);
        }
        strategy.pay(amount);
    }
}
```

Adding a new payment method is literally adding a new `@Component` bean. No factory edit, no switch statement.

### How is Strategy different from Template Method?

Strategy selects the entire algorithm at runtime via composition. Template Method defines a skeleton in a base class and lets subclasses override specific steps via inheritance.

| Aspect | Strategy | Template Method |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Composition (has-a) | Inheritance (is-a) |
| Selection | Runtime, swap freely | Compile time, fixed by subclass |
| Granularity | Whole algorithm | Individual steps |
| Coupling | Loose | Tight (subclass to parent) |

```java
// Strategy: compose at runtime
PaymentContext ctx = new PaymentContext(new UpiPayment());
ctx.checkout(new BigDecimal("5000"));
ctx.setStrategy(new CardPayment()); // swap at runtime
ctx.checkout(new BigDecimal("3000"));

// Template Method: fixed by subclass at compile time
public abstract class LoanProcessor {
    public final void process() {
        validate();
        calculateEmi();  // subclass provides this step
        disburse();
    }
    protected abstract void calculateEmi();
    // ...
}
```

### What are common mistakes with Strategy?

- Using Strategy for a single, fixed algorithm that will never vary. A plain method is simpler.
- Creating a strategy hierarchy when a simple lambda suffices. In modern Java, `amount -> amount.multiply(rate)` beats a named class for trivial logic.
- Forgetting null checks on the strategy reference, which causes `NullPointerException` at runtime.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Strategy is the pattern Rajesh reaches for most often because it directly enforces OCP.
- In a banking system, strategies absorb regulatory changes: new interest rules, new fee types, new payment methods.
- The `Map<String, Strategy>` pattern in Spring replaces the classic Factory plus Strategy combination.
- Strategy works best when the number of algorithms is open-ended. For two fixed variants, a simple if-else is simpler and honest.
